package makers:plugin@1.0.0;

/// How a plugin produces output.
///
/// The interface this replaces had no answer: its example plugin wrote a
/// Graphviz document to *stderr* and the test asserted on stderr. That is
/// not a small wart — it means plugin output cannot be redirected, cannot be
/// written atomically, interleaves with make's own diagnostics under `-j`,
/// and cannot be discovered or cached by the host.
///
/// Outputs here are *declared* in the manifest and *named logically*. The
/// plugin asks for `"compile-commands"`; the host decides that means
/// `./compile_commands.json`, or whatever `--plugin-arg` said, writes it via
/// a temporary file and an atomic rename, and knows afterwards exactly which
/// files this plugin produced. Declaring them buys three things a raw
/// filesystem capability cannot: the plugin needs no write access to the
/// filesystem at all, the artifact set is knowable before the plugin runs
/// (so `make` can report or clean them), and the host can serve a cache hit
/// without instantiating the component.
interface artifacts {
    use types.{error};

    /// An open declared output. Writes are buffered by the host and become
    /// visible atomically at `finish`; a stream dropped without `finish` —
    /// including one abandoned because the plugin trapped, ran out of fuel,
    /// or returned an error — leaves any previous file untouched. A
    /// half-written `compile_commands.json` breaks every editor that reads
    /// it, so partial output is never published.
    resource output {
        write: func(bytes: list<u8>) -> result<_, error>;
        /// Publish. Idempotent; further writes after it fail.
        finish: func() -> result<_, error>;
    }

    /// Open a declared output by logical name. Fails if the name is not in
    /// the manifest's `outputs`, or if `write-outputs` was not granted.
    open: func(logical-name: string) -> result<output, error>;

    /// Where a declared output will land, absolute. Useful for a plugin that
    /// wants to embed its own path in its output, or log it.
    path-of: func(logical-name: string) -> option<string>;
}
